meta {
  name: 10 Delete account (credential half)
  type: http
  seq: 10
}
delete {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/account
  body: none
}
docs {
  ⚠️ **DESTRUCTIVE, and irreversible.** Deletes the caller's own credential, so sign-in stops working.

  **Call this SECOND**, after `1-Provider/9 Delete account` or `2-Customer/10 Delete account`. This
  credential is what authorises those, so deleting it first strands a live profile — carrying the user's
  name, address and phone — that nothing can reach to finish erasing, and no way to sign back in and retry.

  ⚠️ **Takes no email, in the path or the body.** The account is the caller's own `sub` claim, so there is
  nothing here for a caller to substitute in order to delete somebody else's account. Same reasoning as
  `6 Unregister device token` and `8-Notifications`' mark-all-read.

  Also releases the device-token registration (before the credential, so a fault between the two leaves an
  account that can still sign in rather than a deleted one whose device keeps receiving push) and denylists
  the access token you called with, so it stops working immediately instead of staying valid for the rest of
  its 60-minute lifetime.

  Answers:
  - `204` — **whether or not a credential matched.** A deletion that answered differently for a known and an
    unknown address would be an enumeration oracle on the one route every authenticated caller can reach.
  - `401` with no token, or with a token carrying no `sub` claim.
  - `503` if the credential store is unreachable.

  Afterwards, `2 Login` with the same address answers `401`. Idempotent.
}
